{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 16,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.049,
            0.432,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.088,
            0.848,
            0.185
          ],
          "content": "If six congruent equilateral triangles can indeed be placed as shown in Fig. 6.12, then the sides of the resulting hexagon are equal, and their angles are $60+60=120^{\\circ}$ (how?). So what we really need to examine is whether 6 congruent equilateral triangles can fit this way without overlapping and without leaving any gaps around the centre."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.184,
            0.846,
            0.224
          ],
          "content": "We have defined a degree by taking the complete angle around a point to be $3 6 0 ^ { \\circ }$ . So all the angles around the centre should add up to 360°."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.232,
            0.847,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": "? Consider this figure. Will the $7 0 ^ { \\circ }$ angle fit into the gap? What is the gap angle ∠AOI?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.277,
            0.272,
            0.296
          ],
          "content": "We have,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.295,
            0.611,
            0.316
          ],
          "content": "4 0 ^ { \\circ } + 6 0 ^ { \\circ } + 5 0 ^ { \\circ } + 3 0 ^ { \\circ } + 4 0 ^ { \\circ } + 9 0 ^ { \\circ } + \\mathrm { g a p \\; a n g l e } = 3 6 0 ^ { \\circ } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.32,
            0.614,
            0.359
          ],
          "content": "Use this to determine whether the $7 0 ^ { \\circ }$ angle fits the gap."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.359,
            0.614,
            0.417
          ],
          "content": "Thus, if there are angles that add up to 360°, their vertices can be joined together at a single point such that"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.421,
            0.495,
            0.442
          ],
          "content": "(a) the angles do not overlap, and"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.446,
            0.613,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": "(b) they completely cover the region around the point."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.64,
            0.264,
            0.895,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.49,
            0.615,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": "Since each angle in an equilateral triangle is 60°, six such angles add up to 360°. Therefore, six congruent triangles can be arranged as shown in Fig. 6.12."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.576,
            0.832,
            0.601
          ],
          "content": "? In Fig. 6.12 can you explain why AOD, BOE and COF are straight lines?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.612,
            0.846,
            0.654
          ],
          "content": "？ Construct a regular hexagon with a sidelength 4 cm using a ruler and a compass."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.663,
            0.847,
            0.703
          ],
          "content": "We can construct a regular hexagon more directly if we can construct a $1 2 0 ^ { \\circ }$ angle using a ruler and a compass."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.711,
            0.326,
            0.735
          ],
          "content": "How do we do it?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.737,
            0.624,
            0.757
          ],
          "content": "This can be done if we can construct a $6 0 ^ { \\circ }$ angle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.78,
            0.362,
            0.821
          ],
          "content": "If we construct this,"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.402,
            0.759,
            0.557,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.565,
                0.777,
                0.647,
                0.818
              ],
              "content": "we also get this."
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_18.jpg"
        },
